    "## 4. Visualizing Prediction Intervals\n",
    "We plot the predicted band gap along with its uncertainty bounds to see where the model is most confident in its understanding of the physics. \n",
    "\n",
    "**Observation:** For standard methylammonium lead iodide (MAPbI3) systems, the prediction interval is narrow (low aleatoric noise). However, as we move towards complex 'triple cation' or mixed-anion systems (higher material index), the interval widens, reflecting both the lack of data and the increased sensitivity of the band gap to small ionic substitutions."
   ]
   },
   {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Limitations and Scientific Assumptions\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.1. Interval Calibration\n",
    "*   **Homoscedasticity vs. Heteroscedasticity:** Our model assumes heteroscedasticity (noise varies with input). However, the 'Pinball Loss' does not guarantee that 90% of future points will fall in the interval unless the data distribution is stationary, which is rarely true in evolving perovskite research.\n",
    "*   **Under-coverage:** In regions with zero data (e.g., quaternary mixtures), the model may provide an artificially narrow interval if it over-extrapolates from simpler systems, leading to a false sense of security.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.2. Missing Physical Factors\n",
    "*   **Temperature Dependence:** Band gaps shift with temperature due to lattice expansion (Renormalization). This dataset primarily contains room-temperature measurements, so the uncertainty does not account for thermal band-gap fluctuations.\n",
    "*   **Strain Effects:** Epitaxial strain from the substrate can shift the band gap. Without explicit strain or substrate descriptors, this source of variance is lumped into 'Aleatoric' noise, even though it is physically deterministic."
   ],
   "id": "limitations_md"
   }
